From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HOTTY Agent <hotty@neuroplast.io>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:04:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] vello_cpu: flushing waits for the workers without spinning

`MultiThreadedDispatcher::flush` drains the recorded commands with
`record_finished_commands(false)`, which looped on `try_recv` until every
worker had dropped its sender: the main thread spun at full speed for as
long as the workers took to finish the last batch. It now blocks in the
ordered channel's `recv`, which returns the tasks in the same order and
fails once the senders are gone and nothing is buffered, so the loop ends
where it did.

In hotty-blitz painting a 10 Hz dashboard (about 0.45 Mpx of 3.5 Mpx
changing a frame, 8 workers), the painting thread's instructions fell by
39% and the process's by 23%; frame times did not change.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/dispatch/multi_threaded.rs | 14 +++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/dispatch/multi_threaded.rs b/src/dispatch/multi_threaded.rs
index 557076b..a553819 100644
--- a/src/dispatch/multi_threaded.rs
+++ b/src/dispatch/multi_threaded.rs
@@ -302,7 +302,19 @@ impl MultiThreadedDispatcher {
 
     fn record_finished_commands(&mut self, abort_empty: bool) {
         loop {
-            match self.recorded_command_receiver.as_mut().unwrap().try_recv() {
+            // When flushing, block until the next task (in order) arrives or
+            // every worker has hung up. Spinning on `try_recv` here kept the
+            // main thread busy for as long as the workers took.
+            let next = if abort_empty {
+                self.recorded_command_receiver.as_mut().unwrap().try_recv()
+            } else {
+                self.recorded_command_receiver
+                    .as_mut()
+                    .unwrap()
+                    .recv()
+                    .map_err(|_| TryRecvError::Disconnected)
+            };
+            match next {
                 Ok(mut task) => {
                     let num_tasks = task.allocation_group.recorded_commands.len();
                     for cmd in task.allocation_group.recorded_commands.drain(0..num_tasks) {
